<h1>Belt Conveyors: Types, Materials &amp; Selection Guide</h1>
<p class="usa-intro">A belt conveyor is a conveyor that carries products on the top surface of a continuous belt loop. A motor turns a drive pulley that pulls the belt around a tail pulley, and a slider bed or rollers support the belt between them. An industrial belt conveyor system adds the frame, a take-up to hold belt tension, guarding, and controls. Unlike <a href="/conveyor-types/roller-conveyors/">roller conveyors</a>, which need a firm, flat product bottom, belt conveyors carry bags, pouches, small parts, irregular items, and loose bulk material. Belt types range from general-purpose flat belts to cleated, modular plastic, and wire mesh designs for specific jobs.</p>
<h2 id="subtypes-heading">Explore Belt Conveyor Sub-Types</h2>
<ul class="usa-collection">{beltTypes.map((type) => (
<li class="usa-collection__item"><div class="usa-collection__body"><h3 class="usa-collection__heading"><a class="usa-link" href={type.slug}>{type.name}</a></h3><p class="usa-collection__description">{type.desc}</p>{type.tags && <p class="tags">{type.tags.map((tag: string) => <span class="usa-tag">{tag}</span>)}</p>}</div></li>
))}</ul>
<h2 id="how-heading">How Belt Conveyors Work</h2>
<p>A belt conveyor consists of a continuous belt loop, a drive pulley (head pulley) powered by a motor, an idler pulley (tail pulley) that provides tension, and a support structure that carries the belt and products. The belt wraps around the head and tail pulleys, with the top surface (carry side) transporting products and the bottom surface (return side) traveling back empty.</p>
<h3>Belt Support Methods</h3>
<p><strong>Slider bed</strong> conveyors support the belt on a flat plate or wear strips (typically UHMW). This provides a solid surface for precise positioning and is common in packaging applications. <strong>Roller bed</strong> conveyors support the belt on a series of small rollers, reducing friction for longer conveyors and heavier loads. <strong>Troughed</strong> configurations use angled idler rollers to form a U-shape for bulk material containment.</p>
<h3>Drive Systems</h3>
<p>Most belt conveyors use an AC gearmotor driving the head pulley directly or through a chain/belt transmission. <a href="/resources/automated-conveyor-systems/">Variable frequency drives</a> (VFDs) allow speed adjustment. For precise positioning, servo drives provide accurate stop/start control for <a href="/conveyor-functions/indexing-conveyors/">indexing applications</a>. <a href="/resources/conveyor-motors-drives/">Motor sizing</a> depends on belt length, speed, product weight, and incline angle.</p>
<h3>Belt Tensioning</h3>
<p>Proper belt tension is critical for tracking and drive efficiency. Tensioning methods include screw take-up (manual adjustment via threaded rods), gravity take-up (weighted pulley provides constant tension), and automatic take-up (pneumatic or spring-loaded). Insufficient tension causes belt slippage. Excessive tension accelerates bearing and belt wear.</p>
<h3>Belt Tracking</h3>
<p>Belt tracking keeps the belt centered on the pulleys and frame. Crowned pulleys (slightly larger diameter at center) naturally guide the belt to center. Tracking adjustments at the tail pulley correct persistent mistracking. Training idlers (self-aligning rollers) automatically correct belt drift. Proper tracking prevents edge wear, spillage, and belt damage.</p>
