<p>Incline figures are manufacturer guidelines, not guarantees. Product weight, bottom surface, moisture, and dust all change the usable angle, so test with real product. See the <a href="/resources/conveyor-cost-guide/">cost guide</a> for more on pricing factors.</p>
<h2 id="materials-heading">Conveyor Belt Materials</h2>
<h3>PVC (Polyvinyl Chloride)</h3>
<p>The most common belt material for general-purpose packaging conveyors. PVC belts are economical, available in many surface finishes (smooth, rough-top, pattern-top), and usable across normal plant temperatures (check the belt data sheet for the exact range). Standard PVC compounds are not intended for direct food contact. Food-grade PVC exists, but many food plants choose PU instead.</p>
<h3>PU (Polyurethane)</h3>
<p>Preferred for <a href="/industries/food-conveyors/">food and beverage applications</a> because food-grade PU compounds are made to comply with FDA food-contact rules (<a href="https://www.ecfr.gov/current/title-21/chapter-I/subchapter-B/part-177" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">21 CFR Part 177</a>), resistant to oils and fats, and easier to clean than PVC. PU belts offer better abrasion resistance and longer service life than PVC but cost more. Temperature range is similar to PVC. White and blue PU is standard for food lines (blue for visibility in case of belt fragments).</p>
<h3>Modular Plastic</h3>
<p><a href="/conveyor-types/modular-conveyors/">Modular plastic belts</a> consist of interlocking plastic modules joined by hinge pins. Materials include polypropylene (PP), acetal (POM), and polyethylene (PE), all available in FDA-compliant grades. Modular belts excel in <a href="/industries/food-conveyors/sanitary/">sanitary washdown environments</a> because individual modules can be removed for cleaning or replacement.</p>
<h3>Wire Mesh / Metal</h3>
<p><a href="/conveyor-types/wire-mesh-conveyors/">Wire mesh belts</a> use woven or welded metal wire for high-temperature applications (baking, cooling, heat treatment) and applications requiring airflow through the belt. Stainless and high-nickel alloy mesh handle oven and furnace temperatures, with the limit set by the alloy. Carbon steel is lower cost for non-corrosive, non-food applications. Wire mesh belts are also used for <a href="/industries/food-conveyors/cooling/">cooling conveyors</a> where airflow is needed.</p>
<h3>Specialty Materials</h3>
<p>Specialty belt materials include silicone (high-temperature, non-stick), Teflon/PTFE coating (release properties), anti-static formulations, metal-detectable plastics, and reinforced rubber for heavy-duty bulk handling. Material selection depends on product contact requirements, temperature, chemical exposure, and regulatory compliance (FDA, USDA, 3-A).</p>
<h2 id="applications-heading">Belt Conveyor Applications</h2>
<h3>Primary Packaging</h3>
<p>Belt conveyors transport products through primary packaging operations: filling, sealing, wrapping, and labeling. Flat belts provide stable, consistent product positioning for accurate filling and labeling. Precision belt conveyors with servo drives support <a href="/conveyor-functions/indexing-conveyors/">indexing motions</a> for intermittent packaging machines.</p>
<h3>Incline and Decline Transport</h3>
<p>Belt conveyors handle <a href="/conveyor-types/incline-conveyors/">incline and decline transport</a> that roller conveyors cannot. <a href="https://www.dornerconveyors.com/solutions/cleated-belt-conveyor-solutions" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Dorner</a> limits standard flat belts to angles under 20 degrees and rates <a href="/conveyor-types/cleated-conveyors/">cleated belts</a> up to 60 degrees. Patterned and rough-top surfaces extend incline capability without cleats: <a href="https://www.forbo.com/movement/en-gl/products/conveyor-belts/siegling-transilon/top-face-patterns-and-textures/ppi6n3" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Forbo Siegling</a> states certain patterned belts reach 30 degrees without profiles. Sidewall belts with cleats go to vertical.</p>
<h3>Food and Beverage</h3>
<p><a href="/industries/food-conveyors/">Food and beverage plants</a> use belt conveyors for raw ingredient handling, product transport between processing steps, and finished goods packaging. <a href="/conveyor-types/modular-conveyors/">Modular plastic belts</a> dominate wet environments. <a href="/conveyor-types/wire-mesh-conveyors/">Wire mesh belts</a> carry products through <a href="/industries/food-conveyors/bakery/">bakery ovens</a>, fryers, and <a href="/industries/food-conveyors/cooling/">cooling tunnels</a>.</p>
<h3>Small Parts and Components</h3>
<p>Belt conveyors transport small parts, components, and assemblies that would fall between roller conveyor gaps. Flat belt conveyors with side <a href="/components/guide-rails/">guide rails</a> contain small items. <a href="/conveyor-types/vacuum-conveyors/">Vacuum belt conveyors</a> grip flat, lightweight items (labels, cartons, pouches) that standard friction cannot hold.</p>
<h3>Bags, Pouches, and Flexible Packaging</h3>
<p>Flexible packaging (bags, pouches, pillow packs) cannot travel on roller conveyors because they conform to roller gaps. Belt conveyors provide the continuous surface these products require. Gentle-handling belts with soft surfaces prevent puncturing or crushing delicate pouches.</p>
<h2 id="selection-heading">Selecting a Belt Conveyor</h2>
<p>Belt conveyor selection involves matching belt type, material, width, and speed to your product and application requirements.</p>
<p><strong>1. Product characteristics.</strong> Consider product weight (determines belt strength and motor sizing), dimensions (determines belt width), bottom surface (smooth, irregular, soft, rigid), temperature (hot products require heat-resistant belts), and sensitivity (fragile products need soft or cushioned belts).</p>
<p><strong>2. Environmental requirements.</strong> <a href="/industries/food-conveyors/sanitary/">Food contact</a> requires FDA-compliant materials. Washdown environments require sealed components and corrosion-resistant construction. High or low temperatures require appropriate belt materials. Chemical exposure requires compatible belt and frame materials.</p>
<p><strong>3. Incline angle.</strong> Calculate the incline angle from floor rise and horizontal run. Then match the belt surface to that angle: smooth flat belt for gentle slopes (Dorner: under 20°), patterned or rough-top for moderate slopes (Forbo: up to 30° for certain patterns), <a href="/conveyor-types/cleated-conveyors/">cleated</a> for steep runs (Dorner: up to 60°), and sidewall-cleated belts for near-vertical bulk lifts. Steeper runs usually run slower.</p>
<p><strong>4. Speed and throughput.</strong> Calculate required belt speed from throughput and product spacing. Higher speeds require more robust belt construction, larger motors, and attention to product stability at load/unload points.</p>
<p><strong>5. Accumulation needs.</strong> Belt conveyors do not accumulate as easily as <a href="/conveyor-types/roller-conveyors/">roller conveyors</a>. If accumulation is needed, consider a belt conveyor with a <a href="/conveyor-types/mdr-conveyors/">roller accumulation section</a>, or a modular plastic belt with low-friction top surface that allows slip accumulation.</p>
<p><strong>6. Cleaning and maintenance.</strong> Evaluate cleaning requirements and <a href="/resources/conveyor-parts/">maintenance access</a>. <a href="/conveyor-types/modular-conveyors/">Modular plastic belts</a> are easiest to clean and repair (individual modules replace without removing the entire belt). Fabric belts may require more frequent replacement in abrasive or wet environments.</p>
<h2 id="evaluate-heading">How to Evaluate Belt Conveyor Suppliers</h2>
<p>Belt conveyor suppliers fall into two groups: conveyor builders that sell complete frames, drives, and controls, and belting makers that sell belts to those builders and to plants for replacement. Most projects buy from the first group. Ask each supplier for:</p>
<ul>
<li><strong>Published load and speed ratings</strong> for the exact model, width, and length quoted, not a series maximum.</li>
<li><strong>Belt data sheet</strong> naming the compound, food-contact compliance, temperature range, and minimum pulley diameter.</li>
<li><strong>Incline and transition design</strong> (nose-over or Z-frame) with the angle tested on your product.</li>
<li><strong>Guarding and safety</strong> to <a href="https://www.asme.org/codes-standards/find-codes-standards/b20-1-safety-standard-conveyors-related-equipment" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">ASME B20.1</a>, including nip points at pulleys and e-stops.</li>
<li><strong>Belt change and spare parts</strong>: time to replace a belt, and which parts are stocked regionally.</li>
</ul>
<p>See the <a href="/resources/conveyor-manufacturers/">conveyor manufacturers directory</a> for supplier listings.</p>
